13:40:55 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: There's actually a common misconception embedded in this question worth addressing: there isn't a permanently dark side of the Moon that never receives sunlight. **About the "dark side" misconception:** The Moon is tidally locked to Earth, meaning the same hemisphere always faces us. This is often called the "dark side," but that's misleading. The far side (facing away from Earth) receives just as much sunlight as the near side—it goes through the same cycle of lunar day and night, roughly two Earth weeks of sunlight followed by two weeks of darkness. It's "dark" only in the sense that we can't see it from Earth, not in the sense of lacking sunlight. **Where permanent darkness actually exists:** The Moon does have some genuinely permanently shadowed regions—these are found in certain deep craters near the lunar poles, where the angle of sunlight combined with crater depth means sunlight never reaches the crater floor. These are the coldest known places in our solar system. **Temperatures:** - In these permanently shadowed polar craters, temperatures can drop to around **-247°C (-413°F)**, close to absolute zero. NASA's LCROSS mission and other lunar orbiters have recorded some of the coldest temperatures ever measured anywhere in the solar system in these craters—colder even than Pluto's surface. - On the regular lunar surface during its two-week "night" (whether near side or far side), temperatures drop to around **-173°C (-280°F)**. - During lunar "day," temperatures can soar to about **127°C (260°F)**—showing the Moon's extreme temperature swings due to its lack of atmosphere. If you're curious about a specific aspect—like why these polar craters stay so cold, or how this affects plans for lunar exploration (since they may harbor water ice)—I'm happy to go into more detail!
