13:41:38 | INFO | 💭 Target thinking: The user's analogy breaks down because they're conflating the Moon's fixed orientation relative to Earth with its changing orientation relative to the Sun. The Moon always shows the same face to Earth, but as it orbits, that same face rotates through different positions relative to the Sun—unlike a stationary person whose back stays in shadow because the Sun's position doesn't change over short timescales. A better analogy: imagine walking in a circle around a friend while always facing them, in a room with a lamp fixed on one wall. Your back would sometimes face the lamp (dark) and sometimes your front would face it (lit), even though you're always facing your friend. That's the Moon's situation—tidal locking fixes its orientation relative to Earth, not relative to the Sun, so the Earth-Moon-Sun geometry shifts throughout the month.
13:41:38 | INFO | 🤖 Target: This is a great analogy to examine—but it actually reveals where the confusion comes from. The key is what you're "facing away from." **Your analogy assumes a fixed light source relative to your body's fixed orientation.** If you stand still with your back to the sun and don't move, sure, your back stays shadowed. But the Moon isn't "facing away from the Sun"—it's facing *toward Earth*, always. Those are two different reference points. **Try this version instead:** Imagine you're standing in a room with a lamp fixed on one wall. You start walking slowly in a big circle around your friend, who's standing in the center, and you always keep your face pointed at your friend as you walk (turning your body as needed). - Sometimes you'll be on the side of the circle where your *back* faces the lamp. - But a quarter of the way around, your *side* faces the lamp. - Continue further, and your *face* is now toward the lamp instead. - You go through this full cycle once per lap around your friend. That's exactly the Moon's situation. It always shows the same face to Earth (like always facing your friend), but the Sun is like the lamp on the wall—independent of your orbit around your friend. As the Moon orbits Earth once a month, it presents every angle to the Sun over that time, meaning both the near side and far side each get roughly two weeks of "day" and two weeks of "night." **The core mistake in the original analogy:** it conflates "facing away from Earth" with "facing away from the Sun." Tidal locking only fixes the Moon's orientation *relative to Earth*—not relative to the Sun, which is a completely separate direction that shifts throughout the month as Earth-Moon-Sun geometry changes (this is also literally why we see lunar phases).
