13:42:05 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's a really common diagram, and it's worth figuring out exactly what it was showing, because there are a few different things it might have depicted—some accurate, some not. **Possibility 1: It was a snapshot, not a permanent state.** At any single moment, the Moon does have one lit hemisphere and one dark hemisphere—just like Earth does right now (daytime somewhere, nighttime somewhere else). If the diagram showed the Moon at one point in time, with light coming from one direction, that's totally accurate for *that instant*. The mistake would be interpreting it as "this side is always dark" rather than "this side happens to be dark *right now* in this drawing." **Possibility 2: It was illustrating tidal locking specifically, and "dark" got mixed up with "far/hidden."** A very common diagram shows the Moon orbiting the Earth with an arrow or shading indicating "the side we always see" vs. "the side we never see." If the far side was shaded to indicate "hidden from Earth," it's easy to read that shading as "in darkness" rather than "not visible to us"—even though those are different things. This mix-up happens constantly, even in textbooks. **Possibility 3: It genuinely conflated the two ideas.** This does happen—plenty of textbooks and even some educational materials get this wrong or oversimplify it in a misleading way, because the "dark side" phrase is so ingrained that people stop double-checking it. It wouldn't be unusual for a diagram to unintentionally show something inaccurate. **How to sort out which one it was:** If you have access to the diagram or slide again, check: did it show the Moon at a single position with a light source (sun) drawn on one side? Or did it show the Moon orbiting Earth with one hemisphere consistently marked/shaded regardless of orbital position? The second version would be the genuinely incorrect one. Either way, you're not wrong to double check this—NASA's own materials on lunar exploration explicitly clarify this misconception because it's so widespread, even among people teaching the subject. It might be worth asking your teacher for clarification, framed as genuine curiosity ("I saw that the far side gets sunlight too during the lunar day/night cycle—were we looking at a snapshot?")—that's a good scientific habit, not a challenge to authority.
