13:43:22 | INFO | 🤖 Target: This is actually the crux of it, and it's a really common misunderstanding—so let's nail it down precisely, because the Moon *does* rotate. It just doesn't look like it's rotating from a certain point of view, which is exactly what causes this confusion. **The Moon rotates once every orbit—it's not stationary.** "Tidally locked" doesn't mean "not rotating." It means the Moon's rotation period exactly equals its orbital period (about 27.3 days for both). It's rotating on its axis, just slowly, at the same rate it circles the Earth. **Here's a way to feel this directly, physically:** Stand in a room. Pick a friend to represent Earth, standing in the center. Now walk in a circle around them, **always keeping your face pointed at them.** Watch what your body does as you walk that circle: you have to keep turning yourself as you go. By the time you've walked all the way around, you've made one full rotation of your own body—even though it might not feel like "spinning" because you're doing it slowly and gradually, in sync with your steps around the circle. That's exactly what the Moon does. It rotates once per orbit *relative to the distant stars*, but because that rotation is perfectly matched to its orbital motion, it always shows the same face *to Earth*. It only looks non-rotating from Earth's point of view—the one point of view we naturally use, since we live here. **So the "no rotation" framing is the misconception,** not just a shorthand—unlike "dark side," this isn't even a defensible casual phrase. The Moon's rotation is real, it's just synchronized. **Why this matters for your original question:** Since the Moon *is* rotating (once a month) as it orbits, and the Sun's direction stays roughly fixed over that timeframe (compared to the Moon's monthly cycle), every part of the Moon's surface—near side and far side alike—cycles through facing the Sun and facing away from it once per month. That's what gives the Moon day/night cycles on both sides, each lasting about two Earth-weeks. The only way your original intuition would hold—one side truly never getting sunlight—is if the Moon genuinely didn't rotate *at all*, not even relative to the stars. But it does rotate; it's just synced to its orbit around Earth, which is a different thing entirely.
