13:43:53 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Let's try something different—not another analogy, but a direct check against something you've actually observed yourself: **moon phases**. **You've watched the Moon go from full, to half, to a thin crescent, to invisible (new moon), and back again, over roughly a month. Right?** Here's why that observation alone breaks the "one side always lit, one side always dark" idea—without even touching the far side: If one hemisphere of the Moon were *permanently* lit and the other *permanently* dark, then the side facing us (the near side, which we always see) would either be: - Always fully lit, or - Always fully dark, or - Some fixed, unchanging mix of lit/dark region, night after night. But that's not what happens. **The same near side we always see changes from fully dark to fully lit and back, over and over, monthly.** At new moon, the entire near side is in shadow. Two weeks later, at full moon, that exact same near side is completely sunlit. The line between light and dark (the "terminator") visibly sweeps across the *same* hemisphere over the course of the month. That can only happen if sunlight is sweeping across the Moon's surface over time—meaning no single half is fixed as "the lit half" or "the dark half." The illumination pattern is *rotating relative to the Moon's own surface*, which is only possible if the Moon itself is rotating (relative to the Sun) as time passes. **This is something you can verify with your own memory of the sky, no spacecraft, no trust in me required.** Have you ever noticed the full moon and new moon are both looking at the exact same face of the Moon (the one we always see), just lit completely differently? If so, that's direct evidence against a fixed "light side/dark side." Does that land differently, or is there a specific part of this that still feels off?
