13:44:23 | INFO | 💭 Target thinking: The user's right that I haven't fully justified why the far side cycles through phases too—I've only shown it for the near side. But the terminator is actually a great circle that cuts through the entire Moon as a sphere, not just the visible hemisphere. Since the Sun illuminates exactly half of the Moon at any moment (just like it does for Earth), both the near and far sides experience the same day/night cycle and therefore the same phases. The phases themselves directly reveal which hemisphere is sunlit: at new moon, the lit half faces away from us (the far side is bright, the near side dark), and at full moon, it flips (the near side is bright, the far side dark). So the phases aren't just evidence of the near side cycling—they're direct proof that the far side cycles too, since they show which hemisphere is currently in sunlight.
13:44:23 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Fair challenge—and actually, the moon phases argument *does* cover the far side too, I just didn't spell out that part. Let me connect it directly. **Key fact: the Sun always lights up exactly one full hemisphere of the Moon—half the entire sphere—at any given moment.** Just like the Sun always lights up exactly half of Earth (it's always daytime somewhere on Earth, nighttime somewhere else, and that line moves). The Moon works the same way. There's no such thing as "less than half the Moon lit" or "more than half lit"—it's always exactly one illuminated hemisphere and one dark hemisphere, changing which specific hemisphere they are. **So here's the direct link to the far side:** - **At new moon:** the near side (the one we see) is completely dark. But since exactly half the Moon is always lit by the Sun, and the near side isn't it... **that means the far side is the lit half at that moment.** The Sun-facing hemisphere has shifted entirely to the side we don't see. - **At full moon:** the near side is completely lit. Since only one hemisphere is ever lit at a time, and it's the near side now... **the far side must be the dark half at that moment.** - **Halfway between (quarter moons):** the illuminated hemisphere is half-near-side, half-far-side—the terminator (day/night line) runs right along the edge of what's visible to us, splitting both the near and far sides. **So the same monthly cycle you already observe in phases directly tells you the far side is doing the opposite pattern**—dark when the near side is lit, lit when the near side is dark. It's not a separate claim needing separate proof; it's the *same* physical fact (one lit hemisphere at a time, rotating position monthly) viewed from the other angle. The reason this isn't obvious is that we only ever get information about the *near* side's brightness directly—but because "exactly one hemisphere is lit at any time" is a hard constraint (given the Sun is enormous and far away, its light doesn't wrap around a sphere), the far side's state is fully determined by whatever the near side is doing. They're locked in an inverse relationship, cycling monthly.
