ed the people who'd have the clearest expertise on this specific question—it's mainly persisted as folk language among non-specialists, alongside accurate technical understanding existing in parallel. **Here's the actual test, regardless of history:** does the evidence (moon phases you've personally seen, spacecraft photos of the far side, rover missions operating there on solar power) support "always dark" or not? If the direct evidence contradicts it, then no amount of the phrase's age changes that—it just means the phrase outlived its literal accuracy, the way lots of old phrases do.
