13:47:34 | INFO | 🤖 Target: This is actually a really important observation to unpack, because I think there's a subtle mix-up in what those photos are actually showing you. **Every photo you've seen of a crescent, half, or full moon is a photo of the same side—the near side.** All of them. From Earth, we can only ever photograph the hemisphere facing us. So when you see a crescent moon photo, that's the *near side*, partially lit. When you see a full moon photo, that's also the *near side*, this time fully lit. You've never actually seen a photo of the far side with your own eyes in casual life (unless you specifically sought out spacecraft images), so those photos can't tell you anything about whether the far side gets light. **Here's the key distinction your observation is actually running into:** A crescent moon photo does NOT show "half the moon lit, half dark" as two separate hemispheres (near vs. far). It shows **the near side alone, with part of *that same near side* in shadow and part lit**, depending on the Sun's angle that month. The "dark" sliver in a crescent moon isn't the far side peeking through—it's literally part of the visible near side, just not currently catching sunlight. **So what your photos actually demonstrate, correctly:** - Same hemisphere (near side) → different amounts of it lit at different times → because the Sun's angle relative to that hemisphere keeps changing. That directly *supports* what I've been arguing, not the "only one side ever gets light" idea: if the *same* fixed hemisphere is sometimes fully dark (new moon) and sometimes fully lit (full moon), the light hitting the Moon clearly isn't fixed to one hemisphere—it's sweeping across the whole surface over time, near side and far side alike. **A useful mental check:** picture a full moon photo—completely lit disc. That's the *entire visible hemisphere*, 100% in sunlight, at that moment. If "one side is always dark," which side would that even be, in that photo? There's no dark region left to point to—because at full moon, the entire face you can see is lit. That alone shows illumination isn't fixed to a permanent geography on the Moon; it shifts with the Sun's position over the month.
