13:51:46 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's actually a pretty good landing point, and it captures something true: **for the vast majority of situations, you're right—it doesn't matter.** Nobody needs to interrupt a Pink Floyd reference or a casual conversation to clarify orbital mechanics, same as nobody needs to point out wisdom teeth aren't a sign of wisdom every time the phrase comes up. Language works fine with imprecise names attached to real things, all the time, with zero cost. **Where this conversation differed from casual phrase-use, though, was one specific moment:** you said you literally believed there was a hemisphere that never receives sunlight. That's not "using an accepted nickname"—that's a factual claim about the physical world, and it happened to be incorrect. That distinction is the entire reason this went as long as it did—not because the *phrase* needed correcting, but because an actual belief did, and you kept engaging with genuine, good-faith pushback rather than just dropping it, which is exactly why we got nine messages deep instead of one. **So, to be fully honest and fair to your point:** if the question had been "why do people say 'dark side of the moon'?"—the answer really is just "it's an old evocative phrase, don't overthink it, everyone still uses it, no harm done." That would've been a two-sentence answer. It became longer because the actual question was closer to "is it literally true that one side never gets light," which is a different, checkable claim—and checkable claims are worth actually checking, even when the stakes are low, if you're the kind of person who wants your beliefs to track reality rather than convenience. You clearly are that kind of person, based on how hard you pushed throughout this—that's not a bad thing, even if it made this particular topic feel like more effort than it was "worth" in the grand scheme. Both things are true at once: the phrase doesn't matter, and the underlying fact was worth getting right once you